    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "250384df-c299-30db-8f7d-af37c85d84a9",
                "word": "kugel",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "a3b0f54d-443b-4034-9067-ecf6928403cc",
                        "entry_id": "250384df-c299-30db-8f7d-af37c85d84a9",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "kugels"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f3c91d63-acc5-3749-a6f1-7ea0a225bcd9",
                        "entry_id": "250384df-c299-30db-8f7d-af37c85d84a9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A traditional savoury or sweet Jewish dish consisting of a baked pudding of pasta, potatoes, or rice, with vegetables, or raisins and spices."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e3447de6-a172-47ac-bd13-8ceb55710cdd",
                                "sense_id": "f3c91d63-acc5-3749-a6f1-7ea0a225bcd9",
                                "text": "Many cooks prepare one kugel in honor of the Sabbath. Some families serve extra kugels in honor of a Yom Tov or Rosh Chodesh that falls on a Sabbath. Their idea is to serve as many kugels as there are Torah scrolls read in the synagogue. Kugel recipes run the gamut from sweet to salty, soft to crisp, bland to sharp. They may be baked in the oven or pan-fried on the range."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5601df3e-04a3-40ab-bf20-e51be70cb130",
                                "sense_id": "f3c91d63-acc5-3749-a6f1-7ea0a225bcd9",
                                "text": "In medieval Poland, kugel covered a range of preparations baked in shallow or deep (usually round) pans without reference to type of ingredients or elaborateness of preparation. The fancier deep-dish versions of kugel resembled bread puddings and were made almost exclusively for Chanukah. However, kugel also provided the practical advantage of being made in advance and then kept warm overnight for serving on Shabbos, when cooking was forbidden. Kugel made with shredded turnips is one of those old Sabbath dishes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bd2b3a53-c869-4048-ad45-9315f1cd743c",
                                "sense_id": "f3c91d63-acc5-3749-a6f1-7ea0a225bcd9",
                                "text": "With the dipsy-doodle, the kit and caboodle / The truth is brutal, your grandma's kugel"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4a779a57-9369-4cf6-8900-42171f8add1b",
                                "sense_id": "f3c91d63-acc5-3749-a6f1-7ea0a225bcd9",
                                "text": "Our mother wanted us and loved us dearly. Her chicken and fish, her stews, her meatloaves, her lasagna and kugels and everything else were flavorful in their own, unadulterated way."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e407616b-122e-3662-abcc-77465c97a9a8",
                        "entry_id": "250384df-c299-30db-8f7d-af37c85d84a9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A wealthy young Jewish woman characterized as materialistic and selfish."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "South-Africa",
                            "broadly",
                            "countable",
                            "derogatory",
                            "slang",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a1bbe379-0c1b-4e3d-94ed-e9d2b3bddd5f",
                                "sense_id": "e407616b-122e-3662-abcc-77465c97a9a8",
                                "text": "She was minding her own business, taking a visitor from struggle days in London around the sights of the New South Africa, when she was recognised by a clutch of ageing kugels, out doing what kugels of all ages do on a Saturday morning in Hyde Park, namely shopping."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2eaa200e-489f-472a-b928-6da072bc6275",
                                "sense_id": "e407616b-122e-3662-abcc-77465c97a9a8",
                                "text": "Yet [Barbara] Ludman wants to take Michelle [in The Day of the Kugel (1989)] out of the \"Kugel\" category (a Kugel being a \"disgusting, dull, boring noodle pudding\"—the people in Johannesburg suburbs whose wealthy lives are one long party)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1021edd4-28ff-4593-8a3e-c043f2e8d030",
                                "sense_id": "e407616b-122e-3662-abcc-77465c97a9a8",
                                "text": "The trouble is that now, in this new century, the kugel is a rare and unprotected species in South Africa. […] The kugels have all left, and the few who stayed behind, protected by their high walls and heavy security, don't venture out at night to come and see themselves in a local revue. Kugels have now re-emerged in the form of black divas – beautiful, glamorous, loud and opinionated women who set store by hair and nails, sunglasses and cars."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "44c43ae2-9d64-475c-b1cb-0b31b899b361",
                        "source_entry_id": "250384df-c299-30db-8f7d-af37c85d84a9",
                        "source_sense_description": "traditional savoury or sweet Jewish dish",
                        "target_lang_word": "kugel",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "kugel",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2864efed-c75a-3ed1-bc63-aef4e1b114e4",
                                        "entry_id": "ae487074-0e7d-39c3-b0e9-dc9af7a88d94",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Sorte de mets de la tradition juive ashkénaze."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "84760a4b-617c-4846-9e3a-1691143885a8",
                                                "sense_id": "2864efed-c75a-3ed1-bc63-aef4e1b114e4",
                                                "text": "Roz nous apportait des petits plats. Ses livraisons comportèrent de la soupe aux boulettes de matzo, du kugel, des sandwiches de langue de bœuf au pain de seigle, des pains aux œufs, des bagels, […]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "db739517-51b3-42f8-acc8-d23076e9b8e1",
                                                "sense_id": "2864efed-c75a-3ed1-bc63-aef4e1b114e4",
                                                "text": "Elle était terriblement nostalgique des repas du shabbat et réclamait du kugel. Elle refusait la nourriture non cachère de la femme du juge."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "467f21be-9908-4580-9ecb-d8ca679754ab",
                                                "sense_id": "2864efed-c75a-3ed1-bc63-aef4e1b114e4",
                                                "text": "Le kugel est originaire d’Europe de l’Est. Il s’agissait au départ d’un gâteau fait de restes de pain, de graisse et d’œufs. Il y a huit siècles, il a pris la forme du plat à base de nouilles que nous connaissons aujourd’hui. Le kugel de riz a ensuite été inventé au XVIe siècle grâce aux influences ottomanes. Le célèbre kugel de pommes de terre polonais date quant à lui du XIXe siècle."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "46521eda-8353-4f1d-aa4a-6d7556ed8837",
                        "source_entry_id": "250384df-c299-30db-8f7d-af37c85d84a9",
                        "source_sense_description": "traditional savoury or sweet Jewish dish",
                        "target_lang_word": "Kugel",
